235: \((-1,0,1)\), \((1,-1,0)\) in the closed charts. In the ordered
236: curve normal coordinates its tangent weights and normal differences
237: are
238: \[
239:                   w=(0,1,-1),\qquad d=(-2,-1,-1).
240: \]
241: The ordinary product (DZ.13) carries its normal action to
242: \[
243:  Q\longmapsto
244:  (H_t e^{-iw_i\phi/2}H_t^{-1})Q
245:  (H_{l_i-t}^{-1}e^{id_i\phi/2}H_{l_i-t}).            \tag{DZ.15}
246: \]
247: The retained diagonal unit exponentials conjugate this literal
248: formula and give a based homotopy back to it, with the normal metric
249: transported. Normal rescaling carries the higher derivatives as in
250: DZ.2. Both parameter ends and every radial-end physical orbit are
251: closed; the vertex orbit maps are independent of the parameter.
252: 
253: Here is the actual degree calculation with its arrows. On the
254: positive source path put \(U=\cos\chi+j\sin\chi\) and
255: \(E_i=e^{iw_i\phi/2}\). The moved source Hopf representative is
256: \(E_i^{-1}UE_i\). At the normal section \(q_H=1\), the trace is
257: \[
258:  E_i^{-1}UE_i H_t E_i^{-1}H_t^{-1}U^{-1}
259:               H_{l_i-t}^{-1}e^{id_i\phi/2}H_{l_i-t}. \tag{DZ.16}
260: \]
261: For (DZ.12), direct differentiation at the inverse images of
262: \(-i\) gives
263: \(\deg(H_diH_d^{-1})=-d\) and
264: \(\deg(H_d^{-1}iH_d)=d\), in the path--parameter orientation.
265: The factor \(UiU^{-1}\) has degree zero. The positive quaternion
266: volume gives degree \(c e\) for an axis of degree \(e\) and angle
267: \(c\phi/2\). The exact Maurer product boundary terms vanish on
268: the radial ends and the constant central-sign angle ends; the
269: parameter faces cancel. Thus (DZ.16) has normal-meridian degree
270: \[
271:                     I_i=t w_i+d_i(l_i-t).            \tag{DZ.17}
272: \]
273: For the derivative use the universal covering group of stable
274: \(GL^+\), with its integral third class identified by the lifted
275: polar deformation. The chain-rule identity is retained in that
276: group. A left quaternion factor contributes its degree and a right
277: factor its negative. The base rotation has only the \(\phi\)
278: variable. At \(2\pi\) the base and normal Spin endpoints are both
279: \((-1)^{w_i}\), so their product is one. The resulting full
280: closed stable derivative has degree
281: \[
282:                     W_i=t w_i-d_i(l_i-t).            \tag{DZ.18}
283: \]
284: These computations use the same actual endpoint caps as CT.3:
285: take generic normal sections with all vertex coordinates nonzero,
286: connect them by paths interpolating squared moduli and phases in
287: the vertex links, and cap each parameter-independent end by
288: \(D^2_\theta\times S^1_\phi\), constant in its disc variable.
289: The circle changes only phases and preserves the axis-tube
290: inequalities. The vertex traces factor through dimension two.
291: Thus no extra vertex meridian or third derivative class occurs.
292: The change from the coordinate tangent frame to the ambient
293: stable frame extends through each annulus times its normal
294: four-disc, whose third cohomology is zero; its pairing on each
295: such capped trace is zero. This is the same exact frame arrow
296: used in ST.2, now on the closed charts constructed in DZ.1.
297: 
298: Let \(V\) be the actual ordinary-product variation, \(\delta\)
299: the actual ambient stable derivative, and \(D_i,m_i\) the
300: oriented relative cycles and normal meridians. For
301: \(\gamma_j=a_j-a_0=\partial x_j\), the positive-circle boundary
302: identity \(\partial\Delta=-\Delta\partial\) gives
303: \[
304:              \Delta x_j=w_jD_j-w_0D_0 .
305: \]
306: The literal action prism and the chain-rule/Stokes prism then give
307: \[
308:  V(w_je_j-w_0e_0)=I_je_j-I_0e_0,\qquad
309:  w_j\delta_j-w_0\delta_0=W_j-W_0.                    \tag{DZ.19}
310: \]
311: The return fixes the common boundary by the actual product in
312: DZ.2; its integral meridians generate \(H_3\), and \(H_2\) of the
313: common exterior is zero. These are exactly the groups in the
314: three-curve complement of K29, since the curves and their
315: neighbourhood have been carried by the explicit ordinary product.
316: They justify both prism identities as identities of the actual
317: return.
318: 
319: Substituting (DZ.11) gives
320: \[
321:  \begin{split}
322:  I&=(-2(N+2a-3),\,2N+2b-4,\,-2N+4),\\
323:  W&=(2(N+2a-3),\,2-2b,\,-2).
324:  \end{split}                                          \tag{DZ.20}
325: \]
326: In particular \(w_0=0,w_2=-1\) yield
327: \[
328:                   V_{22}=2N-4,\qquad
329:                   \delta_2=2N+4a-4.                 \tag{DZ.21}
330: \]
331: K31's actual ambient-to-single-star product frame map has third
332: class \(e_2\). Carrying that map by the based product above retains
333: its derivative correction \(-V_{22}\); it is not an identification
334: of two unmarked frames. Hence the actual ordinary relative
335: integers satisfy
336: \[
337:  2n_2=\delta_2-V_{22},\qquad m_2=-V_{22}-n_2,
338:  \quad\text{so}\quad
339:            (m_2,n_2)=(-2N+4-2a,\,2a).               \tag{DZ.22}
340: \]
341: 
342: ## DZ.4. The native Cox product and the actual flop
343: 
344: The diagonal native star in the closed charts is literally
345: \[
346:  (z,c_0,c_1)=(a_0,b_0,c_0),\qquad
347:  (z',c'_0,c'_1)=(u_0,v_0,w_0),\qquad
348:                   z'=z^{-1},\quad c'_i=zc_i.
349: \]
350: Its Hopf coordinate is
351: \(q_H=U(z)(c_0+j\overline{c_1})\).
352: The native-to-ordinary boundary arrow is, with the full unit
353: homotopy retained,
354: \[
355:  q_H\longmapsto
356:        (U H_t U^{-1})q_H H_{l_2-t}.                 \tag{DZ.23}
357: \]
358: Its left factor has degree \(2t\) on the Hopf-oriented sphere
359: times the parameter, by the exact Maurer boundary integral of
360: OP.21--23 with exponent \(t\). Its right factor depends on only
361: the radial coordinate and parameter and has third degree zero.
362: Extending the inverse factors over \(D^3\times I\) by the
363: literal cylinder retraction of OR.2 gives endpoint degrees
364: \((-2t,0)\). Consequently the ordinary relative return is the
365: native return followed by the actual pure-left map \(L^{-2t}\).
366: There is no disc-supported factor: these coefficient maps extend
367: over \(D^3\times D^4\). In particular the native right integer is
368: unchanged, and the complete native pair is
369: \[
370:                         (m_2^{\rm nat},n_2^{\rm nat})
371:                                       =(2-2a,\,2a). \tag{DZ.24}
372: \]
373: 
374: The single and doubled seam maps themselves also extend through
375: the flop. In Cox
376: coordinates with auxiliary weights \((1,1,-1,-1)\), take the
377: chart \(z=a_1/a_0,c_i^{\rm chart}=a_0c_i\).
378: For the multipliers \(A,D\) in (DZ.5) or (DZ.8a), the literal Cox lift is
379: \[
380:  (a_0,a_1,c_0,c_1)\longmapsto
381:        (a_0,(AD)^{-1}a_1,Ac_0,Dc_1).               \tag{DZ.25}
382: \]
383: Here the actual height is \(q=a_0a_1c_0c_1\), which this map
384: preserves. Thus even for the full \(q\)-dependent multipliers its
385: inverse is obtained by replacing \(A,D\) by \(A^{-1},D^{-1}\)
386: at the same \(q\); no implicit inverse or constant-unit
387: replacement is needed.
388: It commutes with the auxiliary action, the physical circle and
389: all period translates. It gives (DZ.7) on the original star and
390: extends to the other small resolution by its other quotient
391: chart. Transport the Hermitian metric by this map, then use its
392: positive radial normalization on the common link. Thus the
393: two native product links and their parameter seams are carried
394: by the same actual Cox map, with all its nonunit moduli retained.
395: On each free period representative this is a literal map;
396: equivariance extends it to the whole periodic orbit of curves.
397: 
398: The common-link arrow and its oriented exterior extension are
399: therefore the same explicit maps
400: \[
